 2013, which was $409 billion less than the deficit in fiscal year 2012.\n\nView Document\n\n## Summary\n\nThe federal government incurred a budget deficit of $680 billion in fiscal year 2013, which was $409 billion less than the deficit in fiscal year 2012. The fiscal year that just ended marked the first since 2008 that the deficit was under $1 trillion. As a share of the nation’s gross domestic product (GDP), the deficit declined from 6.8 percent in 2012 to 4.1 percent in 2013. (The deficit was 1.1 percent of GDP in 2007, prior to the recent recession.)\n\nImage: Fiscal Year Totals\n\nTwo unusual factors had large but mostly offsetting effects on the reduction in the deficit from 2012 to 2013. First, a shift in the timing of certain payments reduced the 2012 deficit: About $31 billion in payments that would ordinarily have been made on October 1, 2011, were made instead in September 2011 because October 1 fell on a weekend. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nB U L L E T I N (https://fiscal.treasury.gov/system/files/files/reports-statements/treasury-bulletin/b2014-1.pdf)\nciteturn77search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Economic Policy ... The federal budget deficit declined to $680 billion (4.1 ... $1.089 trillion (6.8 percent of GDP) in fiscal year 2012. ... Together with the $2.5 trillion in ... these measures would bring the total amount of deficit ... President’s fiscal year 2014 budget (released in May 2013), ... percent of GDP over the last five years of the budget ... then begin to decline, falling to 69.8 percent in fiscal year\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHistorical Tables Fiscal Year 2011 (https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/sites/default/files/omb/budget/fy2011/assets/hist.pdf)\nciteturn77search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.8 years ago; Crawled: 16.8 years ago; Table 6.1—COMPOSITION OF OUTLAYS: 1940–2015—Continued ... | Total outlays 